**Amendment (Direct listener and client trust)** · `amend:api:direct-listener` The node serves public operations directly over plain HTTP, without TLS in the process or a required terminator. Public network records need no transport secrecy; submitted public signed material retains its exact signed bytes, alteration fails authentication and repetition grants no authority beyond the author's own retry under (`rule:api:submission`). A certificate, service name and terminator would add deployment components without replacing independent verification of the record. The cost is integrity and availability on the path: a response, status or health answer can be altered or suppressed, and a client that does not independently check the selected run and public evidence is exposed. HTTP success proves neither admission nor publication. This is not a declaration that a private proposal opening is public: realizes{``open:design:proposal-carriage``; This surface enforces the carriage hold and claims no entropy or admitted replacement process.} keeps that unresolved route out of this decision. A front is optional deployment machinery, never a trusted forwarding-metadata boundary. Public listener reachability is distinct from private service reachability under (`rule:intranet:secret-reachability`).

**Amendment (Listener bootstrap)** · `amend:api:listener-bootstrap` The authenticated local start operation receives a public bootstrap tuple of numeric bind address, address family, port, served API edition and permitted operation set, alongside the public per-link cases of (`inv:intranet:explicit-case`). It accepts IPv4 or IPv6 and loopback or a declared public address through the same HTTP listener; an IPv6 socket is explicitly IPv6-only, and serving both families requires two declared binds sharing the same global bounds. No address or port is compiled in, inferred from a hostname or silently substituted after failure. Require a nonzero explicit port and refuse duplicate or unavailable binds before reporting listener-bound. A wildcard bind is an explicit public choice, never a default. The supervisor passes these public start arguments; they are not extra keys in the connection-only JSON of (`rule:storage:configuration`), nor an alternative binding-policy file. Publish the accepted tuple and edition in startup status. A bind serves only its declared operation set, validated against the edition; the set is public bootstrap, not inferred from a forwarded address or request body. While realizes{``open:design:proposal-carriage``; This surface enforces the carriage hold and claims no entropy or admitted replacement process.} stands, startup refuses every non-loopback address, including wildcard binds, whose operation set includes submission. Submission may be enabled only on a numeric loopback bind, checked by the node in the declared address family. The alternative is an operator-declared host-secured path under (`case:intranet:host-secured`); its confidentiality, integrity and far-end authenticity are operator responsibilities that the node cannot infer from a non-loopback address. That alternative is not admitted for this interim. Loopback makes the bind restriction checkable; it neither authenticates the Actor nor protects against another process on the same host, and it does not discharge secret custody or the carriage hold. The same listener uses a declared operation-class endpoint selected from the bounded request line before reading a body. A submission endpoint on a disallowed bind returns OperationNotServed/403 and closes without reading, decoding or recording its private fields, and without sending 100 Continue. Any bytes already read ahead past the refused request line are discarded unparsed and unrecorded when the connection closes; buffering is not permission to decode a refused body. Read-only endpoints expose no submission input type; their selected GraphQL operation must match the endpoint and permitted set before any resolver. This restriction is not authority for the act and does not identify an Actor. A caller can put arbitrary bytes on a socket: the guarantee is no application body read of a refused submission endpoint, not secrecy for arbitrary bytes mislabelled as a public read. The listener cut supplies a control with an unread private body on a public bind and an admitted local counterpart, and a submission pipelined after a read on the same connection with read-ahead discarded at refusal; relaxing the bind guard must fail it. This route admission does not alter complete recording of arguments once an operation is invoked, or any secret-carrier exception. The operator reserves its port against other services and automatic ephemeral allocation when necessary. A deployment may limit new connections per source with an optional packet filter. The node does not treat a source address as an Actor or provide per-source fairness: one source can occupy every public connection slot and repeatedly reacquire it, denying other clients and remote health. This is an accepted availability cost of the first test node, not a guarantee supplied by global bounds. The node independently bounds its accept work and retained resources; availability against single-source exhaustion requires the deployment's filter or intervention.

**Amendment (Listener bounds and refusals)** · `amend:api:listener-bounds` Under (`rule:api:limits`), the served edition's reviewed public limit register supplies every bound below; a missing value prevents listener startup. Bounds are robustness and availability controls, not mathematical verdicts. Reserve a connection slot and an accept-attempt allowance before accepting and creating per-connection state, admit framed lengths before buffer reservation, and check accumulated bytes before each bounded read or parser expansion. Unknown-length bodies are streamed into the remaining admitted byte budget, never collected first. Reject ambiguous HTTP framing before resolver entry. Allocate only within both the individual and global admitted budgets. Deadlines are absolute monotonic deadlines from the named start, checked during each wait and before execution; progress cannot renew them. The execution deadline bounds orchestration, not the time of an uninterruptible commit. A timeout never certifies rollback. Only one request executes per connection at a time; no unbounded pipelining queue is retained. Refusal responses themselves fit the response budget and write deadline; otherwise close without a body. Trace recording retains (`rule:observe:privacy`), including its exceptional debugging cost; these bounds do not claim to change observation policy.

| Public bound | Unit and interval | Exhaustion and connection | Measurement deciding the value before service |
|---|---|---|---|
| listen_backlog | Pending connections per declared listening socket | Configure a finite backlog; a full queue is refused or times out by the kernel, with no promised HTTP response; no user-space overflow queue | Listener cut: effective kernel backlog, declared bind count, burst connections and queue occupancy |
| accept_attempts / accept_window | Maximum accept attempts in one fixed monotonic window across all binds; positive window duration | AcceptRateLimit; suspend accept polling until the next window or a slot is available, whichever is later; no accept-and-close spin. Charge failed attempts as well | Listener cut: bursts, rapid connect/close churn, unsuccessful accepts and CPU per attempt |
| descriptor_reserve | File descriptors reserved for control, observation and shutdown, excluding listening, client and dependency-session descriptors | DescriptorBudget; refuse startup unless the process descriptor limit covers listeners, concurrent_connections, bounded dependency sessions and this reserve by checked addition; suspend accepts on unexpected descriptor exhaustion | Listener cut: measured descriptor peak in addition to bounded store/RPC sessions, service restart and full-slot controls |
| dependency_connections | Maximum concurrent store/RPC acquisition or probe sessions across the process, outside client slots | DependencyConnectionLimit; no new acquisition until a slot is free; dependency unavailability and 503 for affected operations, never recursive unbounded reconnect | Lifecycle cut: startup/reconciliation, failed connections, probes and reconnect concurrency |
| admitted_request_bytes / admitted_response_bytes | Aggregate retained input/decoded request and encoded output bytes across all admitted connections | AggregateByteLimit; reserve before buffer allocation, refuse admission with bounded resource error if possible then close; no hidden waiter queue | Listener and operation cuts: counted simultaneous allocation and parser expansion with retained documents |
| execution_slots | Concurrent resolver executions across all binds | ExecutionSlots; refuse dispatch with resource-exhaustion result if possible then close, no queued resolver; existing work retains its slot until reconciled | Operation cut: supported operation mix and cancellation/commit boundaries |
| connection_state_bytes | Maximum node-owned connection scaffolding bytes per live connection, excluding separately charged request/response bytes | ConnectionStateLimit; reserve before creation or extension, close if the allowance cannot be met; checked product with concurrent_connections defines the global scaffolding ceiling | Listener cut: peak parser/task/socket-wrapper state over supported connection phases and cancellation |
| header_bytes | Total received HTTP request line and headers in octets, framing included | HeaderLimit; 431 then close; no resolver | Listener cut: maximum admitted header shapes and malformed framing at ceiling and one above |
| body_bytes | Wire octets after the header terminator through the body framing terminator, including chunk-size lines, extensions and delimiters, independent of decoded limits | BodyLimit; 413 then close; no resolver. Trailers are unsupported: a Trailer header or nonempty trailer section yields UnsupportedTrailers/400 then close, without interpreting trailer fields | Listener cut: declared act and query envelopes, chunked overhead, trailer refusal and allocation witness |
| request_read | Duration from accept for the first request, first next-request byte thereafter, through complete headers and body | RequestReadTimeout; 408 if possible then close; no resolver | Listener cut: bounded maximum requests, delayed/dripping peer controls and scheduler conditions recorded |
| execute | Duration from resolver dispatch through result or operational cancellation | ExecutionTimeout; resource-exhaustion GraphQL result if delivery remains possible, then close; effect may be unknown | Operation cut: supported reads and commits, cancellation and lost-acknowledgement faults |
| response_bytes | Complete encoded response octets | ResponseLimit; bounded error instead of a partial publication, then close | Operation cut: largest supported exact documents and counted output allocations |
| response_write | Duration from first write attempt to completion | ResponseWriteTimeout; close; no replacement or rollback assertion | Listener cut: maximum bounded response and stalled-reader control |
| concurrent_connections | Live accepted connections across all binds, including idle, reading and writing | ConnectionLimit; stop accepting while slots are full, no application state or promised HTTP body for queued peers | Listener cut: per-connection memory, simultaneous waits and available service resources; one source occupying every slot |
| requests_per_connection | Completed or refused requests on a connection | Last allowed response has Connection: close; no further request executes | Listener cut: reuse cost and fairness under the declared request mix |
| idle | Duration after a response until first next-request byte | IdleTimeout; close without application work | Listener cut: reuse intervals and idle-slot occupancy |
| read_progress / write_progress | Maximum duration without a transferred octet during reading / writing, in addition to absolute deadlines | SlowRead or SlowWrite; close, bounded 408 only for a readable request refusal | Listener cut: stalled and intermittent peers; a trickle never extends the absolute deadline |
| shutdown_drain | Duration from stop signal until all owned operations are reconciled or left explicitly uncertain | DrainExpired; close remaining connections and stop; recovery is mandatory on restart | Lifecycle cut: commit/disconnect/kill boundaries and supported shutdown workload |

The named global admitted budgets are admitted_request_bytes, admitted_response_bytes and execution_slots; connection budgets are concurrent_connections, dependency_connections, listen_backlog, accept_attempts/accept_window and descriptor_reserve. A declared finite per-connection state allowance measured by the listener cut, multiplied by concurrent_connections with checked arithmetic, bounds connection scaffolding separately from those byte reservations; overflow refuses startup. The refusal writer reserves its error bytes from the same output budget or closes without writing. The accept scheduler does not poll a full slot set, so pressure cannot force an unbounded accept/close loop. The operating system owns packet/SYN processing; these are bounds on node work, not protection against network saturation. The listener cut must show its accept counter stays within the declared window under full slots and churn while service/control descriptors remain usable.

The measurement cut records workload counts and canonical byte sizes, hardware/build, load, peak allocation and observed durations, then proposes finite values with evidence and review; this design selects none. Ceiling values are inclusive for bytes and counts, while a deadline reached expires. The same register also retains every CBOR, GraphQL, list and resolver bound already required by (`rule:api:limits`). The listener cut's first edition measures execute, response_bytes and shutdown_drain over its health-only workload; later operation editions remeasure and review those bounds as reads or mutations are added, before serving them. A future commit workload is not a prerequisite for the health-only edition.

**Amendment (Health and lifecycle)** · `amend:api:health-lifecycle` Add a bounded, read-only health query to the public listener's reviewed schema, so a supervisor exercises the same bind and executor that clients use. It returns process-up, listener-bound, store-reachable, chain-reachable, readiness, served edition and a public bounded dependency-status age; unknown and unavailable are distinct from reachable. Before successful bind, only the authenticated local inspect operation and process exit status are available. The lifecycle cut declares and measures a dependency-probe deadline, bounded retry/backoff, and maximum status age in the same public edition register; stale status reads unknown and readiness false. Readiness is true exactly when every declared bind is bound, the edition and all its bounds and operation sets have been validated, every pending reconciliation for that edition is complete, including startup recovery and recovery of an uncertain commit after mid-run store loss, the node is not stopping, and every dependency required by its enabled domain operations is reachable in a status snapshot no older than the declared maximum age. Otherwise it is false. A health-only edition can become ready after its own startup checks without enabling a domain operation. An edition requiring the chain stays not ready when it is unavailable, even if an independently available persisted read can still succeed; per-operation checks remain authoritative. The listener cut must hold reconciliation incomplete with reachable services and observe false, complete it and observe true, then lose the store during a possible commit, restore reachability while reconciliation remains pending and observe false until recovery completes; expire the snapshot and stop to observe false. Dependency reachability alone cannot clear the pending-reconciliation state. Health reads a retained bounded status snapshot; it does not trigger unbounded reconnection work or disclose endpoint, credential, database error or key material. A reachable dependency establishes liveness only, not a valid schema, authenticated epoch or chain finality. Local inspection remains authoritative to the supervisor; a remote health response is unauthenticated evidence under (`amend:api:direct-listener`).

Start the database and chain daemon before the node; initialize the schema with the separate owner task before the runtime role opens it. Establish secret custody and read public bootstrap and the credential set, validate the edition and every bound, then bind; operational dependency unavailability permits a not-ready listener, but malformed bootstrap, unsupported edition, unsafe credentials or an incompatible store identity fails startup. Retry dependency acquisition only within declared bounded backoff and connection budgets. Health remains available while not ready; domain operations return ServiceUnavailable/503 until their required dependencies and recovery checks succeed. Persisted reads may be served only if their declared contract does not require a presently unavailable chain view; readiness never substitutes for per-operation checks. On stop, stop accepting connections and dispatching new domain work, close idle connections, drain owned work under shutdown_drain, then release the runtime sessions. Cancel only where the domain contract permits; recover uncertain commits by their structural identity, never from the HTTP status or a cancelled future. A forced stop may leave uncertainty for startup reconciliation. Stop the node before its services; unexpected service loss makes readiness false, and restored reachability does not make it true while any reconciliation remains pending. Restart, including a scheduled reboot, uses the same start and reconciliation path with the same durable state and credentials; no manual successful-response reconstruction is required.
